      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "The two-coset product formula extends the corrected character χ=φ+b(φ)φ₀ with Eχ(σ)=0. Relative openness gives local constancy and S∩V⊆U ensures Eχ and ε obey the local vanishing conditions. The map Θ(φ)=Eχ+b(φ)ε records the bit on σ. Its restriction to U recovers χ, so both φ and the bit are recovered inside the ambient character count.",
      "review": "An independent reader checked that the bit requires no extra multiplicative factor, and the encoding may fail to restrict to the original φ when the bit is one. In (𝔽₂²)⋊C₄ the four invariant characters map to the four characters of the larger group."
    },
    {
      "id": "QCS-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "A square-zero difference gives the quadratic subgroup bound",
      "anchor": "inputs-quadratic-character-square",
      "statement": "Let U≤V be relatively open of index dividing two and let S be stable under conjugation by V with S∩V⊆U. If the locally constant additive 𝔽₂-character group C(V) has at most n elements, then C(U) has at most n². This applies to the quadratic datum of any prime p dividing the allowed ramification modulus N.",
      "hypotheses": "Γ a topological group, U,V subgroups and n a natural number. In the arithmetic specialization choose Ω an algebraic closure of ℚ, any abelian datum A, p prime, p|N. U is the group for A.sup(AbF.quad p) inside the dyadic tower.",
      "definitions": [
        "At index one U=V. At index two J pulls a character back under u↦σ⁻¹uσ. Conjugation by σ² is inner in U, so J²=1 on characters.",
        "D=1+J has D²=0 in characteristic two. Its kernel is the fixed space and C(U)/ker D≃im D embeds into ker D.",
        "For S the union of inertia away from N, conjugation preserves S. Since p|N, inertia off N fixes the pth roots and lies in the quadratic fixing subgroup."
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-quadratic-character-cardinality"
      ],
